An entry with true flip contribution $f_{ir}$ raises its row's lower cost by $f_{ir}$ and reduces its upper cost by $p_i-f_{ir}$. Mean pooling can have integer $0<f_{ir}<p_i$, so one query contributes to both proofs. Table~\ref{tab:joint-example} shows how changing the retained row reduces the evidence union from twelve entries to seven. Appendix~\ref{app:deduplicated} realizes these contributions as mean scores and proves minimality.

